// `char_count` was deleted on 2026-10-23: `len` on text answers its question now. The old name
// says so, and says where the *byte* count went too -- a reader reaching for `char_count` wants
// `len`, but a reader who had been relying on the old byte-counting `len` wants `byte_len`, and
// that one gets no message at all because it still compiles. Which is exactly why the swap had to
// be swept with the compiler rather than announced.
//
// It is the only entry in `swapped_meaning_fn`, the fourth and narrowest of typecheck.c's tables
// for a name that is gone. The others: a lost prefix (`renamed_builder_fn`), a plural a variadic
// absorbed (`absorbed_by_variadic_fn`), a name describing its argument where a conversion is named
// for its result (`renamed_for_result_fn`). This one is none of those -- nothing was renamed,
// merged or deleted in spirit; two names traded what they answer.
fn main() {
    print(char_count("héllo"))
}
